<%
  page_data(
    title: "Exchange authorisation code for access token",
    error: false,
    caption: "API Developer documentation",
    caption_size: 'l'
  )
%>

<p class="govuk-body">
The process is shown in this diagram which continues from the
<%= govuk_link_to("receive authorisation results",
                  api_documentation_authentication_page_path("receive-authorisation-results"),
                  no_visited_state: true) %> step.
</p>

<%#
  Mermaid code for the image

sequenceDiagram
    actor User as Client
    participant Vendor as Application
    participant RIAB as Authorisation API

    Vendor->>RIAB: POST /oauth/token
    RIAB->>RIAB: Validate request and generate token
    RIAB-->>Vendor: 200 OK with token payload
%>

<%= image_tag("documentation/authorisation/exchange-code-for-token.png", width: "700px", alt: "Exchange authorisation code for access token sequence diagram") %>

<p class="govuk-body">
When you receive the authorisation code, you must exchange this for an access token <strong>within 10 minutes</strong>.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
Do this via a HTTP POST to our token endpoint.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
You must authenticate the request with <%= govuk_link_to("HTTP Basic Authentication", "https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc7617/") %>
using your client ID and secret.  This is added as a HTTP header in which you Base64 encode your credentials in the form <code>client_id:client_secret</code>.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
For example, if your client ID was <code>client123</code> and your secret was <code>supersecretvalue</code> then your Basic Authentication header would be this:

<pre>
<code>
Authorization: Basic Y2xpZW50MTIzOnN1cGVyc2VjcmV0dmFsdWU=
</code>
</pre>

</p>

<%= govuk_warning_text(text: "Do not include your client ID or client secret in the body of the request.") %>


<p class="govuk-body">
Include the other parameters in the request body, not as additional request headers.
</p>

<h4 class="govuk-heading-s">3.1 Example request</h4>

<pre>
<code>
  curl -X POST <%= @api_base_url %>/oauth/token \
  --user "client123:supersecretvalue" \
  -H "content-type:application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  --data \
  "grant_type=authorization_code\
  &redirect_uri=[YOUR-REDIRECT-URI]\
  &code=[AUTHORIZATION-CODE] \
  &code_verifier=[CODE-VERIFIER]"
</code>
</pre>

<%= govuk_table do |table|
  table.with_head do |head|
    head.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "Parameter")
      row.with_cell(text: "Description")
    end
  end
  table.with_body do |body|
    body.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "grant_type")
      row.with_cell(text: "The OAuth 2.0 grant type. Currently the only acceptable value is <code>authorization_code</code>".html_safe)
    end
    body.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "redirect_uri")
      row.with_cell(text: "The same redirect URI you used to call the authorisation endpoint.")
    end
    body.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "code")
      row.with_cell(text: "The authorisation code you received from us in the previous step")
    end
    body.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "code_verifier")
      row.with_cell(text: "Your #{govuk_link_to("PKCE", "https://oauth.net/2/pkce/")} <code>code_verifier</code>".html_safe)
    end
  end
end %>

<p class="govuk-body">
The response contains the access token as a JSON payload.
The access token is the equivalent of your password to access the API as the organisation that has authorised the request.  As such you should store it securely.
</p>

<h4 class="govuk-heading-s">3.2 Example response</h4>

<p class="govuk-body">
A successful response will return HTTP status code <code>201</code> to indicate your token was created and a JSON payload containing the access token
</p>

<pre>
<code>
{
  "access_token": "QGbWG8KckncuwwD4uYXgWxF4HQvuPmrmUqKgkpQP",
  "token_type": "Bearer",
  "expires_in": 31536000
}
</code>
</pre>

<h4 class="govuk-heading-s">3.3 Error responses</h4>

<p class="govuk-body">
An unsuccessful request will a HTTP status error code and a JSON payload containing an <code>error</code> value, for example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
{
  error: "invalid_grant"
}
</code>
</pre>

<%= govuk_table do |table|
  table.with_head do |head|
    head.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "Error scenario")
